.TH PQP "1" "2026-07-27" "pqp 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pqp \- quantum-resistant GPG emulation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pqp
[\fI\,GLOBAL OPTIONS\/\fR] \fI\,COMMAND\/\fR [\fI\,ARGS\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBpqp\fR is a CLI tool that emulates GPG's key management, encryption,
signing, and file format, but using post-quantum asymmetric algorithms
(ML-KEM / Kyber and ML-DSA / Dilithium) instead of RSA/ECC.

Keys are stored in \fI~/.local/share/pqp/keys/\fR (XDG data directory),
falling back to \fI~/.pqp/keys/\fR for backward compatibility.  Secret
keys are encrypted on disk with Argon2id + XChaCha20-Poly1305.  All
commands accept \(oq\-\(cq or omit the input positional argument to read
from stdin.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Do nothing; print a dry-run message to stderr and exit with status 0.
Useful for testing command parsing or previewing which command would be
executed.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help information.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
\fBpqp\fR reads \fI~/.config/pqp/pqp.toml\fR (XDG config directory) if
present, falling back to \fI~/.pqp/pqp.toml\fR for backward compatibility.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\fBPQP_HOME\fR
If set, overrides the XDG paths entirely.  Keys will be stored under
\fI$PQP_HOME/.pqp/keys/\fR and config read from \fI$PQP_HOME/.pqp/pqp.toml\fR.
This is useful for running multiple isolated pqp instances.

.TP
\fBHOME\fR
Used as fallback when XDG directories cannot be determined.
Precedence (lowest to highest): code defaults < config file < CLI flags.

Example:
.PP
.EX
[gen\-key]
kem = "ML\-KEM\-1024"
dsa = "ML\-DSA\-87"

[encrypt]
compress = "gzip"

[export]
format = "pqp"
compress = "none"
.EE
.PP
A fully commented sample with pros/cons for every value is installed at
\fIshare/doc/pqp/pqp.toml.sample\fR or available in the source tree.
Config file support mirrors CLI commands.  New options added to the CLI
must also be added to the config file system.

.SH COMMANDS
.SS gen\-key
Generate a PQ key pair (Kyber + Dilithium).
.PP
.B pqp gen\-key \-u\ \fI\,USER\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fI\,USER\/\fR
User ID for the key (required).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-passphrase\fR
Store the secret key without passphrase encryption.  Use for automated
workflows where interactive password prompts are not possible.
.TP
\fB\-\-kem\fR \fI\,VARIANT\/\fR
KEM algorithm variant.  Default: \fBML\-KEM\-768\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBML\-KEM\-512\fR
AES\-128 equivalent security (NIST Level 1).  Fastest and smallest keys.
Suitable for high-volume ephemeral operations.
.TP
\fBML\-KEM\-768\fR
AES\-192 equivalent security (NIST Level 3).  Best balance of security,
speed, and size.  Recommended for general-purpose use.
.TP
\fBML\-KEM\-1024\fR
AES\-256 equivalent security (NIST Level 5).  Maximum quantum resistance.
Largest keys (\(~2\(mu ML\-KEM\-768).  Use for highest security.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-dsa\fR \fI\,VARIANT\/\fR
Digital signature algorithm variant.  Default: \fBML\-DSA\-65\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBML\-DSA\-44\fR
NIST Level 2.  Smallest signatures (\(~2.4 KB).  Fastest signing.
.TP
\fBML\-DSA\-65\fR
NIST Level 3.  Good balance of size (\(~3.3 KB) and speed.
.TP
\fBML\-DSA\-87\fR
NIST Level 5.  Largest signatures (\(~4.6 KB).  Maximum protection.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-encoding\fR \fI\,ENC\/\fR
Encoding for armored export of the key.  Default: \fBBASE64\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBBASE64\fR
Standard base64.  Widest compatibility.
.TP
\fBASCII85\fR
~25% more compact than base64.  Less universal.
.RE

.SS list\-keys
List keys in the keyring.
.PP
.B pqp list\-keys [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,FORMAT\/\fR
Output format.  Default: \fBtable\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBtable\fR
Formatted columns with headers.  Most readable for humans.
.TP
\fBno\-headers\fR
Space-separated values, one per line.  Ideal for scripting with
cut/awk/grep.  Fields: key-id user-id date type kem-algo sign-algo.
.TP
\fBjson\fR
JSON array.  Self-describing field names.  Best for programmatic use.
.RE

.SS encrypt
Encrypt a file for a recipient using a KEM + symmetric encryption scheme.
.PP
.B pqp encrypt \-r \fI\,RECIPIENT\/\fR \-o \fI\,OUTFILE\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR] [\fI\,INFILE\/\fR]
.PP
If \fI\,INFILE\/\fR is omitted or \(oq\-\(cq, reads from stdin.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recipient\fR \fI\,ID\/\fR
Recipient key ID or user ID (required).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,FILE\/\fR
Output file path.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-sign\fR
Sign the ciphertext with the sender's private key.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-signer\fR \fI\,ID\/\fR
Signer key ID or user ID (requires \fB\-\-sign\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-compress\fR \fI\,ALGO\/\fR
Compression applied to plaintext before encryption.  Default: \fBzstd\fR.
The algorithm is stored in the file so decrypt auto-detects it.
.RS
.TP
\fBnone\fR
No compression.  Fastest.  Use when input is already compressed.
.TP
\fBzstd\fR
Zstandard.  Best speed/ratio trade-off.  Recommended default.
.TP
\fBgzip\fR
GZIP.  Slightly better ratio than zstd on some data, slower.
.TP
\fBbzip2\fR
BZIP2.  Better ratio than gzip on text.  Significantly slower.
.TP
\fBlzma\fR
LZMA.  Best compression ratio.  Very slow.  Use for archival.
.RE
.PP
Output file extension: \fI.pqp.enc\fR.

.SS decrypt
Decrypt a file.
.PP
.B pqp decrypt \-o \fI\,OUTFILE\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR] [\fI\,INFILE\/\fR]
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,FILE\/\fR
Output file path.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-passphrase\fR
Use empty passphrase.  Skips the prompt for automated workflows.
.TP
\fI\,INFILE\/\fR
Input .pqp.enc file.  Omit or use \(oq\-\(cq for stdin.

.SS sign
Sign a file with a private key, producing a detached signature.
.PP
.B pqp sign \-u \fI\,USER\/\fR \-o \fI\,OUTFILE\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR] [\fI\,INFILE\/\fR]
.PP
The file is hashed with SHA3-256 and the hash is signed with Dilithium.
Output file extension: \fI.pqp.sig\fR.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fI\,ID\/\fR
Signing key ID or user ID.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,FILE\/\fR
Output signature file path.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-passphrase\fR
Skip the passphrase prompt (uses empty passphrase).

.SS verify
Verify a detached signature.
.PP
.B pqp verify \fI\,SIGNATURE\/\fR [\fI\,INFILE\/\fR]
.TP
\fI\,SIGNATURE\/\fR
Path to the .pqp.sig signature file.
.TP
\fI\,INFILE\/\fR
Path to the original file.  Omit or use \(oq\-\(cq for stdin.

.SS export
Export a public key in armored or PQP self-describing format.
.PP
.B pqp export \fI\,KEY_ID\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.TP
\fI\,KEY_ID\/\fR
Key ID or user ID to export.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,FILE\/\fR
Output file path.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,FORMAT\/\fR
Output format.  Default: \fBarmor\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fBarmor\fR
ASCII-armored PEM-style (\(oq\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN PQP PUBLIC KEY...\(cq).
File extension: \fI.pqp.asc\fR.  Widest compatibility.
.TP
\fBpqp\fR
Self-describing AlgorithmList::EncodedPayload format
(e.g. \fBML\-KEM\-768+ML\-DSA\-65+ZSTD+BASE64::<payload>\fR).
File extension: \fI.pqp.pub\fR.  Embeds KEM and DSA variant names
for interchange without sidecar metadata.
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-compress\fR \fI\,ALGO\/\fR
Compression for PQP format.  Ignored for armor.  Default: \fBzstd\fR.
Same values as encrypt's \fB\-\-compress\fR.

.SS import
Import a public key from an armored file, PQP format, or URL.
.PP
.B pqp import [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR] [\fI\,INFILE\/\fR]
.PP
Auto-detects format (armor or PQP).  The key is stored in the local
keyring.  Confirmation is prompted before importing unless
\fB\-\-dont\-ask\-confirmation\fR is given.
.TP
\fI\,INFILE\/\fR
Armored key file.  Omit or use \(oq\-\(cq for stdin.
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fI\,URL\/\fR
URL to fetch the armored key from.
.TP
\fB\-\-dont\-ask\-confirmation\fR
Skip confirmation prompt.

.SS remove\-key
Remove a public key (and optionally private key) from the keyring.
.PP
.B pqp remove\-key \fI\,KEY_ID\/\fR [\fI\,OPTIONS\/\fR]
.TP
\fI\,KEY_ID\/\fR
Key ID (16-hex directory name) or user ID.  When a user ID is given,
all keys with that user ID are removed.
.TP
\fB\-\-dont\-ask\fR
Skip the confirmation prompt before removing.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\-private\-key\fR
Also delete the encrypted secret key on disk.  Without this flag, only
the public key and metadata are removed.

.SH KEY IDENTIFIER RESOLUTION
All commands that take a key reference (\fBencrypt \-r\fR, \fBencrypt \-u\fR,
\fBsign \-u\fR, \fBexport\fR, \fBimport\fR) accept either a key ID
(16-hex-char directory name) or a user ID (the \fB\-\-user\fR string
provided during \fBgen\-key\fR).  Lookup checks the directory name first,
then matches \fBuser_id\fR in \fBpqp.conf\fR metadata.

.SH FILE FORMAT
All encrypted and signed files use the format:
.PP
.EX
AlgorithmList::EncodedPayload
.EE
.PP
The AlgorithmList is a canonical string of one or more algorithm identifiers
joined with \(oq+\(cq.  The encoding algorithm is always the final component.

Examples:
.PP
.EX
ML\-KEM+XChaCha20\-Poly1305+BASE64
ML\-KEM+XChaCha20\-Poly1305+ZSTD+BASE64
ML\-DSA+SHA3\-256+BASE64
.EE
.SS Encrypted files (.pqp.enc)
The decoded payload contains, in order:
.PP
.RS
1.  KEM ciphertext (Kyber encapsulated key)
2.  Symmetric nonce (24 bytes for XChaCha20-Poly1305)
3.  Symmetric ciphertext + authentication tag
4.  Optional Dilithium signature (if \fB\-s\fR was used)
.RE
.SS Signature files (.pqp.sig)
.RS
1.  SHA3-256 hash of the original file
2.  Dilithium signature over the hash
.RE

.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/pqp/pqp.toml\fR
Global configuration file (XDG config).  Optional.  Fallback:
\fI~/.pqp/pqp.toml\fR.  Overridden by \fBPQP_HOME\fR.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/pqp/keys/\fR
Key storage directory (XDG data).  Each key pair is stored in a subdirectory
named by its 16-hex-char key ID.  Fallback: \fI~/.pqp/keys/\fR.
Overridden by \fBPQP_HOME\fR.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp.conf\fR
Key metadata (TOML format): key ID, creation date, user ID,
algorithm identifiers.  Fallback: \fI~/.pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp.conf\fR.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp-public.der\fR
Public key bytes (raw DER).  Fallback: \fI~/.pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp-public.der\fR.
.TP
\fI~/.local/share/pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp-secret.der\fR
Secret key bytes, passphrase-encrypted with Argon2id +
XChaCha20-Poly1305.  Fallback: \fI~/.pqp/keys/\fIKEYID\fB/pqp-secret.der\fR.
.TP
\fI.pqp.enc\fR
Encrypted file output.
.TP
\fI.pqp.sig\fR
Detached signature output.
.TP
\fI.pqp.asc\fR
ASCII-armored public key (export format).
.TP
\fI.pqp.pub\fR
PQP self-describing public key (export format).

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success.
.TP
1
General error (invalid arguments, I/O failure, crypto failure, etc).

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Generate a key pair
.B pqp gen\-key \-u "alice@example.com"
.TP
Encrypt a file for a recipient
.B pqp encrypt \-r alice \-o secret.pqp.enc doc.pdf
.TP
Decrypt a file
.B pqp decrypt \-o doc.pdf secret.pqp.enc
.TP
Sign and verify
.B pqp sign \-u alice \-o doc.pdf.pqp.sig doc.pdf
.br
.B pqp verify doc.pdf.pqp.sig doc.pdf
.TP
Export and import keys
.B pqp export alice \-o alice.pqp.asc
.br
.B pqp import alice.pqp.asc

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpg (1)

